@@ -4,16 +4,16 @@
 
 ### Upgrade steps
 - **Breaking: the first stage is now called `demo`; `environment: dev` is refused.** The stages are exactly `demo`, `pilot` and `prod`. A config that says `environment: dev` stops at start with "environment: dev was renamed to demo; write environment: demo (allowed values: demo, pilot, prod)" (shown beside any other problem found in the same file, and also for `Dev`, `DEV` and spellings with spaces, which are refused all the same); any other value (`Demo`, `staging`, `uat`, an empty value) is refused too, and values are never tidied or guessed. To upgrade: (1) change `environment:` in your config to `demo`; (2) if your code imports `backends.table.DEV_ONLY`, import `DEMO_ONLY` instead (same six kind pairs, no old name kept); (3) a project label such as `uat` is not a stage: map it to one stage in your own config.
-- **One question for the stage guards.** The pilot and prod checks (`deferred.py`, `registry.py`, `app.py`) now ask one function, `hadavin_sdk.stages.is_live(environment)`, which treats anything that is not exactly `demo` as live; the prod-only rules (no local key file, a retention-agreed anchor) ask `hadavin_sdk.stages.is_prod(environment)`, which is true for `prod` and for any value that is not a stage. `STAGES`, `is_live` and `is_prod` live in the new leaf module `hadavin_sdk/stages.py`, not in `config.py`; the list `deferred.LIVE` is removed.
+- **One question for the stage guards.** The pilot and prod checks (`deferred.py`, `registry.py`, `app.py`) now ask one function, `hadavin_sdk.stages.is_live(environment)`, which treats anything that is not exactly `demo` as live; the prod-only rules (no local key file, a retention-agreed anchor) ask `hadavin_sdk.stages.is_prod(environment)`, which is true for `prod` and for any value that is not a stage. `STAGES`, `is_live` and `is_prod` live in the new small module `hadavin_sdk/stages.py`, not in `config.py`; the list `deferred.LIVE` is removed.
 - **Redactor, `demo` and `pilot` are common words now.** The redactor no longer sweeps the words `demo` and `pilot` as a name (`demo` and `pilot` were added to its common words in this change; `prod` was already there), but a secret that reads `demo` or `pilot` is still swept in every copy of that exact spelling.
 - **Redactor, what is and is not masked for `demo` and `pilot`.** What is and is not masked (pinned by `tests/test_stage_names.py` through both `redact` and `redact_text`, not `_sweep` alone): a tenant or customer named `Pilot` or `Demo` is masked where an id label such as `tenant_id` or `customer_id` gives it (`tenant_id=Pilot`, `customer_id: Demo`; the lower-case `tenant_id=pilot` too), but the same word standing alone is not swept, so `Pilot and Demo again` stays readable; a label without `_id` is not read by the tenant rule at all, so `tenant: Pilot` and `customer=Demo` are not masked anywhere; an ordinary tenant name is masked in every copy; a secret that reads `demo` or `pilot` (`password=pilot`) is masked in every copy of that exact spelling, because the redactor sweeps every copy of a secret that is 4 characters or longer (`demo` has 4, `pilot` has 5) and skips a common word only when it is an identifier.
 - **No stage is stored yet.** The first change that stores one must map an old `dev` value to `demo` on read, for display only and never for a guard decision, and add a test through that real read site.
-- **An id may not be a stage word or a common word.** `hadavin_sdk.stages.check_id` (and `check_tenant_id` for tenants) refuses these 29 words as an id, compared after NFKC, removal of Cf characters, case folding and trimming: `admin`, `api`, `app`, `db`, `debug`, `default`, `demo`, `dev`, `development`, `error`, `false`, `info`, `local`, `main`, `master`, `none`, `null`, `pilot`, `prod`, `production`, `public`, `root`, `stage`, `staging`, `test`, `true`, `user`, `users`, `web`. They are the three stages (`demo`, `pilot`, `prod`), three more stage words (`test`, `development`, `production`) and every word on the redactor's common-word list. That list now lives in one place, `hadavin_sdk/commonwords.py`, imported by both the redactor and `stages.py` (a test pins that it is one list). The error says "may not be a stage word" or "may not be a common word that the redactor does not sweep" and does not echo the value (inside a model that hides input in errors, as `config._Strict` does).
+- **An id may not be a stage word or a common word.** `hadavin_sdk.stages.check_id` (and `check_tenant_id` for tenants) refuses these 29 words as an id, compared after the redactor's text clean-up (see the entry on why, below): `admin`, `api`, `app`, `db`, `debug`, `default`, `demo`, `dev`, `development`, `error`, `false`, `info`, `local`, `main`, `master`, `none`, `null`, `pilot`, `prod`, `production`, `public`, `root`, `stage`, `staging`, `test`, `true`, `user`, `users`, `web`. They are the three stages (`demo`, `pilot`, `prod`), three more stage words (`test`, `development`, `production`) and every word on the redactor's common-word list. That list now lives in one place, `hadavin_sdk/commonwords.py`, imported by both the redactor and `stages.py` (a test pins that it is one list). The error says "may not be a stage word" or "may not be a common word that the redactor does not sweep" and does not echo the value (inside a config model that hides input in errors).
 - **Id field types.** The config field types are `config.TenantId`, `OrgId`, `CustomerId`, `AccountId` and `UserId`, one for each id label the redactor reads (`tenant_id`, `org_id`, `organisation_id`, `organization_id`, `customer_id`, `account_id`, `user_id`). The refusal applies to ids of every source, including the databases of the demo repos (for example `ask-your-data`), wherever the ids are registered.
-- **Why an id may not be one of these words.** The redactor's labelled rule masks the value in `tenant_id=prod` whatever the value is, so that copy is hidden even for a tenant named `prod`. What the redactor does not do is sweep other copies of a value on its common-word list, because `HTTP 500 in prod` must stay readable: an id that is one of those words would keep its unlabelled copies in clear. Refusing the words as ids closes that. "Any spelling" means: Unicode NFKC (a full-width `prod` or a roman-numeral letter becomes plain letters), then the invisible format characters (Unicode category Cf: zero-width space, soft hyphen, word joiner) removed, then case folding and cutting surrounding white space. The first two steps are the redactor's own; the case folding and the cut are added here. Not covered: other invisible or look-alike characters (variation selectors, Cyrillic letters that look Latin).
+- **Why an id may not be one of these words.** The redactor's labelled rule masks the value in `tenant_id=prod` whatever the value is, so that copy is hidden even for a tenant named `prod`. What the redactor does not do is sweep other copies of a value on its common-word list, because `HTTP 500 in prod` must stay readable: an id that is one of those words would keep its unlabelled copies in clear. Refusing the words as ids closes that. "Any spelling" means: look-alike forms made plain (a full-width `prod` or a roman-numeral letter becomes plain letters), invisible characters removed (zero-width space, soft hyphen, word joiner), capitals made lower case and outer spaces cut. The first two steps are the redactor's own function; the last two are added in `stages.fold_name`. Look-alike letters from other alphabets (Cyrillic letters that look Latin) are not turned into Latin letters, so such an id is refused for its characters instead (next entries).
 - **Stage scan: `test`, `development` and `production` are now stage words.** `tests/test_stage_names.py` and the id check treat these three as stage words beside `demo`, `pilot` and `prod`, because they are the usual names of a stage in other projects' configs: a config, doc or CLI flag that says `environment: test` or `--env production` would bring back a second stage vocabulary. The stages themselves are unchanged (exactly `demo`, `pilot`, `prod`). The data-split lines of the demo repos that say `"test"` are listed one by one in `tests/stage_exemptions.tsv`.
-- **Limits of the id check.** No id is created or stored on this branch (no config, store, CLI or backend field holds one), so the check guards no live path yet: the first change that adds such a field must use the types above in a `_Strict` model, and `tests/test_tenant_ids.py` walks `src`, `demos` and `tools` and fails on a field, attribute or parameter named like an id that does not. The check runs only inside pydantic models that use the types (`model_construct` skips it; a dataclass or TypedDict is not validated).
-- **What the id check does not refuse.** An id of 1 or 2 characters that is not on the list (`xy`; `db` is on the list and is refused), because the label rule does not read it; an all-digit id shorter than 6 (masked where labelled, but its other copies are not swept); an id that has a control character (category Cc, such as a NUL or a bell) or a space inside it (`de\x00mo`, `d emo`), which the fold does not remove; and other look-alike or invisible characters (variation selectors, combining marks, Cyrillic or Greek letters that look Latin, the Turkish dotted I). Free text such as `tenant_id=Pilot` in a log is still masked where the label gives it, and a name such as `demos`, `prod-1` or `preprod` is an ordinary id.
+- **Limits of the id check.** No id is created or stored on this branch (no config, store, command-line or backend field holds one), so the check guards no live path yet. The five id types are ready for use and are wired when the first config or identity field named like an id (`*_id`) is added; until then `tests/test_tenant_ids.py` searches `src`, `demos` and `tools` and fails on a field, attribute or parameter named like an id that is not checked. The check runs only inside config models that use the types (it is skipped when a model is built without validation, and a plain data class is not validated).
+- **An id the redactor cannot mask is refused.** The redactor reads the value after `tenant_id`, `customer_id` and the other labels only when it is at least 3 characters long and made of the letters a to z, digits, hyphen and underscore; it copies an all-digit value elsewhere only from 6 digits. `check_id` now refuses an id of 1 or 2 characters (`xy`), an all-digit id of fewer than 6 digits, and an id with any other character: a control character such as a NUL or a bell, a space, a dot, an accented letter, or a Cyrillic letter that looks Latin. Before, such an id was accepted and its other copies stayed readable in logs. The two numbers are the redactor's own (`commonwords.SWEEP_MIN_LENGTH` and `SWEEP_MIN_DIGITS`), not copies. To upgrade: if you register ids, give them only the characters above; a name such as `demos`, `prod-1` or `preprod` is still an ordinary id. Free text such as `tenant_id=Pilot` in a log is still masked where the label gives it.
 - **Code-quality scanner fixes (SonarCloud, an automated code-quality scanner); one public name removed.** `guard.runmap.PARTIAL_ESCAPE` is gone. If your code imports it, call `guard.runmap.partial_escape_start(text, pos)` instead; it returns the start position as a number (or None), not a match object, so replace a `.search(...)` call and its `.start()` with it. The old pattern looked for a cut-off percent code such as `%C3` at the end of a text and slowed down sharply on a long run of them (about 65 s for 40,000 codes in a row, measured 2026-11-06); the new function takes milliseconds. `guard/http.py` now holds the cloud-metadata addresses that no tool may reach as one documented table `METADATA_ENDPOINTS` (the same six addresses as before; the other refused ranges are unchanged).
 - **K5-core, redaction behaviour you may notice.** The producers of text that reaches a model, a vendor screen, a card or a log line (listed in `guard/producers.py`, each tested by `tests/test_k5_round9.py` with a percent-, entity- and assignment-encoded secret) go through one function, `guard.runmap.redact_text`, which also undoes percent, HTML-entity, backslash-u, `\u{41}`, `%u0041`, backslash-x, backslash-U (eight digits) and three-digit octal escapes (not one- and two-digit octal or named escapes: `\1` and Windows paths would decode wrongly) and NFKC to a fixed point before it redacts: a secret written as `%41KIA...`, `&#65;KIA...` or `password%3Dx` is masked in those places, not only in tool results. Not on that list: the text of the ask-your-data demo's own ledger and command line (see its README). A text nested deeper than `defaults.SCRUB_MAX_LAYERS` (8) layers is not shown. A tool result is redacted whole up to 262,144 characters before it is cut; when it IS cut (at that cap, or again after a short handed-out secret was replaced by the longer marker), the last 8,192 characters of the result are dropped so a token that straddles the cut leaves no fragment (`defaults.CUT_MARGIN`; a PEM key is about 1.7 KB for RSA-2048, 3.2 KB for RSA-4096 and 6.4 KB for RSA-8192). Cost: redacting a hostile text at the cap takes one to four seconds on a laptop (one pass per distinct view); a call that has spent `defaults.REDACT_BUDGET_S` (6 s) before a further view is refused (`InputTooLarge`, nothing shown), and the perf step bounds it. A card text is sanitised and has commands withheld first and is redacted once, last (and once more with tags deleted, real placeholders and line breaks kept, when markup split a key from its value; that view is used only when it finds a secret value the text pass did not). A card summary is shown whole or replaced by a short note (never cut); a summary whose inputs are too large to redact says so. In a case bundle, camelCase credential keys (`clientSecret`, `dbPassword`), numbers and nested values under a credential key are masked too. The Kubernetes source defaults (`K8S_*`) stay in `defaults.py`, under their own header, with every other default.
 - **K5-core, stated limits of redaction.** A secret value that itself contains a plain space, `;`, `,` or `&` (for example `password="my secret phrase"`) is masked only up to that character, by the redactor's own definition of a value; and a form-encoded `;` or `&` inside a value (`password%3Dab%3Bzq9`) is ambiguous between one secret and a key and value followed by more text (see SECURITY.md). A masked value nested deeper than the layer cap, or more than 256 values that need pairing across decoded views, refuses the text (`InputTooLarge` or `TooDeep`) instead of showing it.
